  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1142 PM CDT THU APR 16 2026\n\nOne more day of \"status-quo\" conditions are expect today, before the \nhighly anticipated trough and cold front start working into the \nregion by Saturday afternoon. Much to our benefit, guidance is \nstarting to paint a slightly wetter scenario through Sunday when it \ncomes to total rainfall amounts. At this point, any rain is \nwelcomed, but this will hardly be a drop in the pan for what we need \nto break this ongoing drought. In general, locations can expect to \nsee between 0.25 and 0.5 inches of rain, with even less amounts in \nthe areas with highest drought deficits. The rest of the week will \nbe dry, with eyes turning to next weekend at the potential for more \nrainfall then.  \n\nTemperatures are still expected to drop some 15-20 degrees behind \nthe cold front on Sunday, with highs ranging in the upper-60s to low-\n70s. Conditions on Sunday may even approach Red Flag Warning \ncriteria given strong winds and low MinRH values. However, some \nuncertainty remains on how low RH values will drop, so that will be \nsomething we'll keep an eye on the next few days. Otherwise, \nconditions will gradually start to warm, with highs reaching back \ninto the mid-80s by Thursday. \n\n/44/",
